AUDIO: 'Andre Karpathy, the former head of AI at Tesla, recently joined Anthropic to fix Claude\'s biggest problem. This is Claude\'s ability to remember past conversations and not make the same mistake twice. So, I decided to dig deeper and see exactly how Karpathy thinks about this issue. And it turns'

[00:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'out the solution is actually quite simple, and it already exists in Claude. It\'s their feature dreams. And the reason that you\'ve never heard of it and never used it is that it\'s only for enterprise companies. So, how can you recreate this feature yourself? Well, in'

[00:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'this video, I\'ll provide a simple step-by-step overview of how you can create your own version of dreams so Claude never makes the same mistake twice. But before we do that, we need to get a deeper understanding of the problem and how Karpathy thinks about'

[00:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'it. And then at the end of this video, I\'ll cover something that the creator of Claude code said that relates to this topic that is extremely misleading. And if you listen to him, you will waste time and money. So, getting into part one, the problem, how Karpathy sees it,'

[00:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and the fix. To restate the issue, one of the biggest problems people face when using Claude is that they repeat themselves over and over again, and Claude continues to make the same mistakes. So, here\'s how Karpathy thinks about it. And if this sounds too complex, don\'t worry, I\'ll simplify it'

[01:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'right after. &gt;&gt; I feel like when I\'m awake, I\'m building up a context window of stuff that\'s happening during the day. But I feel like when I go to sleep, something magical happens where I don\'t actually think that that context window stays around. I think there\'s some process of distillation into weights of my brain.'

[01:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'We don\'t have an equivalent for all that in large language models. They\'re always like restarting from scratch where they were. &gt;&gt; Now, in this interview, he mentioned two problems. The first one is that Claude often starts from scratch without any context. This is a problem that you\'re'

[01:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'pretty familiar with. But the second problem is he discusses the process of taking short-term contextual information and storing it into long-term contextual information that AI won\'t forget. And he compares this process of taking'

[01:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'short-term memory into long-term memory similar to how a human brain works. When we sleep, we dream, and we bring short-term memories into our long-term memories. So, how do you solve these two problems? Well, the first problem, Claude starting from scratch, you\'ve'

[01:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'likely already implemented solutions to help with this. The simplest version is having a single long-running chat for a specific task, and this is something that I often do. I have a pin chat, one of them\'s for accounting, so whenever I\'m talking about my finances, I go'

[02:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'directly there. This is a simple solution, but it does help. A slightly more advanced version is you leverage a claw.md file, which gets loaded at the start of every new conversation to teach key concepts to the system. And we\'ll'

[02:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'cover that more in a little bit, as well as this rules feature that you likely haven\'t heard of. Or the most advanced solution, and you may be familiar with Karpathy\'s own concept of this, which is LLM knowledge bases. And this is the process of where you ingest files into your local system to help teach it how'

[02:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you operate. Now, this is a concept that I\'ve covered at length on my channel, and one video has over 780,000 views. So, if you search Andrej Karpathy Claude on YouTube, I\'ll be one of the first videos there. Or if it\'s easier, I link'

[02:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that video below, and that\'ll go through the LLM knowledge bases. Now, that\'s the first problem, and we\'re going to dive into that more in a little bit. But how do we solve the second problem of taking the context that Claude got during the day, and then saving it so it\'s'

[02:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'long-term memory, so it doesn\'t forget it in the future. Now, looking back at Karpathy\'s interview, he hinted Claude needed some sort of dream state where it takes your current short-term memory, cleans it up, and then distills it into'

[03:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'long-term memory. And after doing some research, it turns out that this already actually exists in Claude. Now, I mentioned this earlier, but there is a dream feature. And so, on screen, you can see the documentation for how it works, but in plain English, it does'

[03:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'three key things. The first is it takes your existing memory plus up to 100 of your past session transcripts, reads all of it, and builds a brand new reorganized memory. Then, if there are any duplicates or things in your past'

[03:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'memory, it\'ll merge them and clean it all up. Then, it\'ll look for any memories that contradict each other, and then pick the winner. One thing from their design structure is that you can also steer the dream. They have an instructions field where you can tell it what you want it to focus on, and what'

[03:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you want to preserve. Okay, so technical stuff aside, how should you actually think about this? Essentially, it just looks at what you did, analyzes it, and cleans up your Claude memory so it\'s smarter and fresher the next day. And the reason that you don\'t know about this and why you haven\'t tried is that'

[03:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'it runs through the API, which costs extra on top of whatever you pay, and it\'s designed for companies running teams of agents, not necessarily for me and you, which are not these massive enterprise companies. Unless you are running an enterprise company, in which'

