AUDIO: 'industry term that I\'m unfamiliar, instead of just pretending I know what they mean, which I used to do, I\'ll ask Granola in chat, and it gives me a definition on the to And then after the call, I\'ll use Granola\'s MCP to connect directly with Claude Code, which lets me'

[05:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'make more informed decisions and identify the real problems my customers and clients are facing that I may have never actually realized or addressed. I personally recommend Granola to basically everyone I work with, so I\'m excited to partner with them and offer'

[06:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'my community 100% off their first month. So, click the first link in the description to get that all set up. You are going to be happy that you did. Part two is enable your memory system. The first part of creating a dream-like state is making sure we\'re properly'

[06:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'saving memories into our system. So, if you\'re on a recent version of Claude Code, they have a memory feature that is on by default. If you want to look at the setting, open your terminal and in whatever folder you\'re working on, type Claude and then {slash} memory and'

[06:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you\'ll see the configuration details. This feature stores memory files on your computer, so let\'s actually go and look at these. And the reason that we\'re doing this is it will help us understand how we should set up our own dream-like system to complement our existing'

[06:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'memories. So, when you look at the memories folder, you\'ll have a memory.md file, which is just a file that points to other files. And then on those other files, that\'s where the details of the actual memory sits. So, on the left you'

[06:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'can see the memory.md and this is really just a reference file. And then on the right, you can see an actual memory that\'s associated with it. So, the general structure that they have is a parent memory.md file and then sub files'

[07:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'with all the details about what it wants Claude to actually remember. But, that\'s just part of your Claude\'s memory. The next thing is your Claude.md file. Think of this memories.md'

[07:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and your Claude memories as things that just happen in your day. And then Claude.md is the equivalent of what you decide to write down on paper so you don\'t forget it. And if you haven\'t created a Claude.md file yet, don\'t worry, we\'ll create it in the next'

[07:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'section. But, the key with both this memory.md and the Claude.md file is they get loaded in at the start of every session. So, what that means is if you start a prompt and you say, "Hey, this is Austin." it\'ll also see all of your memory context and all of your Claude.md'

[07:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'context. Simply put, it\'s like copy and pasting both files and putting it at the start of the prompt that you just sent. On screen, you can see a table from Anthropic stocks comparing these two features and how they relate. So, you'

[07:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'now have a deeper understanding of how this all works, which is necessary for the next part where we\'ll create a dream state. Part three is to start dreaming. Going back to what Karpathy mentioned earlier, the beauty of how people sleep is that humans just automatically'

[08:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'remember things. It just happens for them, right? We\'ve all experienced this. We\'re all humans. So, what we want to do is create a system that analyzes our history, reviews our memory and claw.md files, and suggest improvements'

[08:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'automatically, a lot like a human sleeping and dreaming at night. To do this, we\'re going to create /dream, an orchestration skill, which is a fancy name for a skill that calls multiple skills. Creating this actual dream skill'

[08:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'is very simple, but first we need to create the three skills that this will reference. The first is session analysis, then improve memory, then send result. So, let\'s start off by creating session analysis. For each of these, I\'m'

[08:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'going to walk through the prompt that creates these because it\'s important to know what\'s going on, but don\'t worry to take any screenshots or anything. You can just use the buildpartner.ai plugin and run /buildpartner:build space dream, and we\'ll walk you through'

[08:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'this step by step. And to get that plugin, that is linked below. So, what this skill does is it looks at your entire conversation history, which it has access to because Claude code automatically saves your conversation history to local files. Currently, the'

[09:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'memory feature we were talking about before just decides what\'s important, but this looks at your entire conversation history. It reads the raw record of what you actually did and pulls out patterns your memory missed. This is a gold mine for information'

[09:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'about how your system can start learning better. Then at the end of the skill creation prompt, we also take in an argument called dream. By passing in this argument, it will create an overview of the most useful memories, which will then bring into the next step'

[09:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'of this process. The reason that I did it like this is that you may want to reuse this same skill in other skills in the future. For example, if I want to see where I\'m spending my time, I add an argument, and the one that we\'re going to use is dream. So, once you create the whole thing, you would write {slash}'

[09:47] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'session analysis space dream. And it tells the skill that you want to analyze it for memories that you want to bring to your long-term memory, which will format it in a way that we\'ll use in the next step of this process. And the reason that I added as an argument'

[09:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'instead of the default is the session analysis skill I may want to use for other workflows in the future. For example, if I wanted to see where I\'m spending my time, I could add another argument called time analysis. Then I would write {slash} session analysis'

