[10:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'skill, we\'re going to take the output of that and pass it through the {slash} improve memory skill. What this does is it looks at your memory and your claw.md files, as well as the analysis from the previous step. Much like the existing dreams feature that Anthropic already'

[10:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'built, it\'ll merge any duplicates, and it\'ll fix any contradictions that exist in these files. It\'ll then suggest upgrades to your claw.md files, so it follows the exact format that Anthropic uses for their memory files that I covered earlier. This is a way to keep'

[10:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'your claw.md file light, and all of the details actually live in separate subfiles. Well, now, one of the problems with just having memories and using a claw.md file is that this is a global configuration for your project. But, we really want to get more specific where'

[11:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'needed. And this brings us to something I literally just found out about when I was researching this video, which makes a ton of sense to me. So, there\'s a concept called rules, where you can have specific memories or instructions that only load when you\'re working on a'

[11:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'specific part of a project. So, for example, if I have a project called my life, and then within that I have an accounting folder, I can have specific accounting rules file that only loads in when I\'m working on my accounting stuff. Then, let\'s say I have a wife folder,'

[11:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you could have wife rules in there that are only specific when you\'re working on things with your wife, which is probably a very extensive file. Again, these are local rules you don\'t want to make global because it\'ll cloud the'

[11:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'contextual window. If you\'re working for things in the wife folder, that doesn\'t necessarily care about the things in the accounting folder. So, zooming out, essentially what we\'re doing is we\'re looking at all of our conversation history and identifying places to improve the system. And some of those'

[11:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'things are obvious, but some of them are not as obvious. So, what we do is we create a file called memory improvement overview. And this is where it\'ll document any of the changes it\'ll auto-approve, and then it\'ll flag anything that you as the human has to'

[12:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'improve for your system. Think of this like Claude identifying ideas that it thinks you should fix, but you\'re the one who\'s finally signing off on it. This is the final human sign-off that makes sure that the system is going in the direction that you want. Then the'

[12:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'last skill that\'s needed is send results. This is personally how I prefer setting up all of my automations. I have a company automations channel within my Slack, and that\'s where all of my automations go. I prefer having things all in one place, so I know where to'

[12:33] [scene:talk]
